Lab 8: Integrating with Apache Kafka
Objectives
Understand Apache Kafka's role in high-throughput, fault-tolerant messaging systems
Configure Apache Camel to produce and consume messages from Kafka topics
Implement event-driven messaging patterns using Camel-Kafka integration
Verify Kafka's message delivery guarantees and reliability features
Prerequisites
Java JDK 11+ installed
Apache Maven 3.6+
Docker and Docker Compose installed
Basic understanding of Apache Camel routes
Basic Kafka concepts (topics, producers, consumers)
Setup Requirements
Create a new directory for the lab:
mkdir camel-kafka-lab && cd camel-kafka-lab
Initialize a Maven project:
mvn archetype:generate -DgroupId=com.example -DartifactId=kafka-camel -DarchetypeArtifactId=maven-archetype-quickstart -DinteractiveMode=false
Add required dependencies to pom.xml:
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
        <artifactId>camel-core</artifactId>
        <version>3.20.0</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
        <artifactId>camel-kafka</artifactId>
        <version>3.20.0</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.kafka</groupId>
        <artifactId>kafka-clients</artifactId>
        <version>3.4.0</version>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
Lab Tasks
Task 1: Set Up Kafka Environment
Objective: Launch a local Kafka cluster using Docker

Create a docker-compose.yml file:

version: '3'
services:
  zookeeper:
    image: confluentinc/cp-zookeeper:7.3.0
    environment:
      ZOOKEEPER_CLIENT_PORT: 2181
  kafka:
    image: confluentinc/cp-kafka:7.3.0
    depends_on:
      - zookeeper
    ports:
      - "9092:9092"
    environment:
      KAFKA_ZOOKEEPER_CONNECT: zookeeper:2181
      KAFKA_ADVERTISED_LISTENERS: PLAINTEXT://localhost:9092
      KAFKA_OFFSETS_TOPIC_REPLICATION_FACTOR: 1
Start the services:

docker-compose up -d
Verify the services are running:

docker ps
Expected Outcome: You should see both Zookeeper and Kafka containers running.

Task 2: Create a Kafka Producer Route
Objective: Implement a Camel route to produce messages to a Kafka topic

Create a Java class KafkaProducerRoute.java:

import org.apache.camel.builder.RouteBuilder;

public class KafkaProducerRoute extends RouteBuilder {
    @Override
    public void configure() throws Exception {
        from("timer:foo?period=5000")
            .setBody().simple("Message at ${date:now:yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss}")
            .to("kafka:test-topic?brokers=localhost:9092");
    }
}
Create a main application class KafkaApp.java:

import org.apache.camel.main.Main;

public class KafkaApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Main main = new Main();
        main.configure().addRoutesBuilder(new KafkaProducerRoute());
        main.run();
    }
}
Run the application:

mvn compile exec:java -Dexec.mainClass="com.example.KafkaApp"
Expected Outcome: The application will send a message to the "test-topic" every 5 seconds.

Task 3: Create a Kafka Consumer Route
Objective: Implement a Camel route to consume messages from a Kafka topic

Create KafkaConsumerRoute.java:

import org.apache.camel.builder.RouteBuilder;

public class KafkaConsumerRoute extends RouteBuilder {
    @Override
    public void configure() throws Exception {
        from("kafka:test-topic?brokers=localhost:9092")
            .log("Received message: ${body}");
    }
}
Update KafkaApp.java to include both routes:

main.configure().addRoutesBuilder(new KafkaProducerRoute());
main.configure().addRoutesBuilder(new KafkaConsumerRoute());
Run the application again.

Expected Outcome: You should see log messages showing both the produced and consumed messages.

Task 4: Implement Fault Tolerance
Objective: Configure Kafka for reliable message delivery

Modify the producer route to enable acknowledgments:

.to("kafka:test-topic?brokers=localhost:9092&requestRequiredAcks=all");
Configure the consumer for manual offset management:

from("kafka:test-topic?brokers=localhost:9092"
    + "&autoOffsetReset=earliest"
    + "&allowManualCommit=true")
    .log("Received message: ${body}")
    .to("kafka:test-topic?brokers=localhost:9092"
        + "&groupId=test-group"
        + "&autoCommitEnable=false");
Add error handling:

onException(Exception.class)
    .log("Error processing message: ${exception.message}")
    .handled(true);
Expected Outcome: The system will now provide better message delivery guarantees and error handling.

Task 5: Test Message Reliability
Objective: Verify Kafka's delivery guarantees

Stop the consumer application while messages are being produced
Restart the consumer after a few minutes
Observe that all messages are processed (no messages lost)
Expected Outcome: The consumer should process all messages, including those sent while it was offline.

Troubleshooting Tips
If Kafka connection fails, verify Docker containers are running:
docker-compose ps
Check Kafka logs:
docker-compose logs kafka
For message visibility issues, use the Kafka console consumer:
docker exec -it kafka-camel_kafka_1 kafka-console-consumer \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic test-topic \
  --from-beginning
Conclusion
In this lab, you successfully:

Set up a local Kafka environment using Docker
Created Camel routes to produce and consume Kafka messages
Implemented fault-tolerant configurations for reliable messaging
Verified Kafka's message delivery guarantees
This integration pattern enables building robust, event-driven systems that can handle high-throughput scenarios while maintaining data integrity.

Cleanup
